# Intent: Fix the adaptive governor at the root (integration 2: the overlays were invisible on the unit because the governor sat at its flat look 68 percent of a 12 minute run: at the flat level it restored one step, the overlays came back with one full-screen redraw of 60 to 120 ms, and that one slow frame shed it straight back). (1) Hysteresis: it sheds only when at least 3 of the last 10 frames were slow (over 45 ms), and restores one level only after 2 s with no slow frame. (2) Grace: for 1.5 s after an overlay starts, the overlays come back, or a level is restored, only 6 slow frames in 10 shed, so the first draw never sheds. (3) Cheap unhide: when the overlays come back nothing is redrawn at once; mo_unhide_step marks the fixed areas and the particle boxes about 6000 pixels a tick, spread over several frames, and the ladder restores the particles in two steps (30 then 50 percent) before 100. (4) A floor: the ladder is now glow off, scanlines off, particles to 50 percent, particles to 30 percent (a requested overlay is still recognizable), then the flat look; while any overlay runs the governor stops at 30 percent and only an emergency (the 99th percentile of frame time over 100 ms for 5 s in a row) goes on to the flat look. The status tool keeps its numbers. Host tests replay scripted frame traces (calm, sustained slowness, a restore spike, the floor, the emergency, a 12 minute run) and show it never oscillates; the host renderer pictures are byte for byte the same.
# Placement: unit (the governor and the overlays run on the robot with no host)
# Upstream: firmware/main/hal/maipai_fx_core.h, maipai_overlay_core.h and maipai_fx.cc (ours, patches TA02, TA05, O01, O02)
# Check after a flash: self.overlay.set stormy heavy for 12 minutes, then self.overlay.status: governor level stays at or above the floor level 4 and the sheds count stays small with restores following them; scripts/fw-serial.py snap shows the overlay in every snapshot; CRT and glow on give the same result with those two shed first.

